1a) Study on Virtualization Types and Tools
Aim: Study virtualization, its types, and associated tools.
Steps:

Understand virtualization: Creating a virtual version of a server, desktop, storage, OS, or network resources.
Study Hardware Virtualization:
Full Virtualization (guest OS unmodified) → Tools: Oracle VirtualBox, VMware Workstation/ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM+QEMU.
Para-Virtualization (guest OS modified) → Tools: Xen, older VMware, KVM.
Type 1 (Bare-metal) Hypervisors → VMware ESXi, Hyper-V, Xen, KVM.
Type 2 (Hosted) Hypervisors → VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels.

Study OS Virtualization (containers sharing kernel) → Tools: Docker, LXC/LXD, Podman, Containerd.
Study Server Virtualization → Tools: VMware ESXi, Proxmox, Hyper-V, XenServer, KVM.
Study Storage Virtualization → Tools: VMware vSAN, IBM Spectrum, Ceph, OpenFiler.
Study Network Virtualization → Tools: VMware NSX, Open vSwitch, Cisco ACI, GNS3.
Study Desktop Virtualization → Tools: Citrix, VMware Horizon, Microsoft RDS.
Study Application Virtualization → Tools: Microsoft App-V, Docker, Turbo.net.
Note the result: Study completed successfully.

1b) Review of Linux Commands
Aim: Study and execute basic Linux commands inside a VirtualBox VM.
Steps:

Start Ubuntu (or any Linux) VM in Oracle VirtualBox.
Open terminal and practice:
ls – list files/directories
cd – change directory
pwd – print working directory
mkdir – create directory
rm / rm -r – delete file/directory
cp – copy files
mv – move/rename
touch – create empty file
cat – display file content
nano – edit file
grep – search pattern
ps aux – list processes
top – real-time resource usage
kill <PID> – terminate process
df -h – disk free space
du -sh – directory size

Verify each command works and note the output.
Note the result: Commands studied and executed successfully.

2a) Creating a VM and Adding Storage, Memory, and Network
Aim: Create a VM and configure storage, memory, and network in Oracle VirtualBox.
Steps:

Open Oracle VM VirtualBox Manager → Click New.
Enter Name (e.g., KESHO_2A), Type: Linux, Version: Ubuntu (64-bit) → Next.
Allocate Memory (e.g., 4 GB; do not exceed 50% of host RAM) → Next.
Create a new virtual hard disk → Dynamically allocated → Size (e.g., 20 GB) → Create.
Go to Settings → Network → Adapter 1: Enable → Attached to: Bridged Adapter (or Host-Only) → OK.
(Optional) Install Ubuntu ISO and complete OS installation.
(Optional performance test) Write a Java program to sum digits, run on both host (Windows) and guest (Ubuntu), compare execution times.
Note the result: VM created and configured successfully.

2b) VM–VM Ping and VM–Native Ping
Aim: Check connectivity between VMs and between VM and host using ping.
Steps:

Set both VMs’ network adapters to Host-Only Adapter (same network, e.g., vboxnet0).
Power on both VMs. Find IPs:
In Linux: ip a or ifconfig
In Windows host: ipconfig

Disable firewalls:
Linux: sudo ufw disable (or sudo iptables -F)
Windows: Enable “File and Printer Sharing (Echo Request – ICMPv4-In)” in Windows Firewall.

Test VM–Native:
From VM → Host: ping <host_IP>
From Host → VM: ping <VM_IP>

Test VM–VM:
From Ubuntu → Kali: ping <Kali_IP>
From Kali → Ubuntu: ping <Ubuntu_IP>

Confirm successful replies (no packet loss).
Note the result: Connectivity verified successfully.

3) Cold Migration of VM from One Host to Another
Aim: Perform cold migration (powered-off VM) using OVA export/import.
Steps:

Create a test file inside the VM (e.g., a Java file) to verify later.
Shut down the VM completely.
In VirtualBox: Select VM → File → Export Appliance.
Choose the VM, select OVA format, choose destination path → Export.
Copy the .ova file to the destination host (USB, network, etc.).
On destination host: File → Import Appliance → Browse to the .ova → Import.
Start the imported VM and verify the test file still exists (cat kesho.java or similar).
Note the result: Cold migration completed successfully; data and configuration retained.

4a & 4b) Installation of Citrix XenServer + Live Migration using XenCenter
Aim: Install XenServer and perform live migration of a running VM using XenCenter.
Steps (Installation – 4a):

Download XenServer ISO from Citrix.
Create bootable USB (Rufus/Ventoy).
Enable VT-x/AMD-V in BIOS; set USB as first boot device.
Boot and install: Select disk, configure network (prefer static IP), set root password, install tools.
Reboot; note the console IP address.

Steps (Live Migration – 4b):

Install XenCenter on a Windows machine.
Add first XenServer (Server → Add → enter IP, username, password).
Create a new VM: Right-click server → New VM → Choose template → Name → Select ISO → Create.
Install a second XenServer the same way and add it to XenCenter.
Ensure shared storage is available (required for live migration).
Right-click the running VM → Move VM → Select the second XenServer → Finish.
Observe the VM appear on the second server with zero downtime.
Note the result: XenServer + live migration completed successfully.

5a) Installation of KVM and Creation of Virtual Instances
Aim: Install KVM and create virtual machines using virt-manager.
Steps:

Check hardware virtualization: egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo (should be ≥ 1).
Install packages:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients bridge-utils virt-manager
Add user to groups: sudo adduser $USER libvirt and sudo adduser $USER kvm. Log out/in.
Start and enable libvirtd: sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd.
Open virt-manager → Right-click QEMU/KVM → New.
Choose “Import existing disk image” (or Local install media).
Download Cirros image → Give path → Set memory/CPU → Name VM → Finish.
Start VM and log in (Cirros default credentials).
Verify with virsh list.
Note the result: KVM installed and VM created successfully.

5b) KVM – Create Images from ISO, Resize, Convert
Aim: Create VM from ISO, resize disk image, convert formats.
Steps (Create from ISO):

In virt-manager → New → Local install media (ISO).
Select Ubuntu Server ISO → Set memory/CPU → Create disk (or use qemu-img create -f qcow2 ubuntu_vm.qcow2 10G).
Complete Ubuntu installation inside the VM.

Steps (Resize):

Shut down the VM.
Resize image: qemu-img resize ubuntu_vm.qcow2 +5G (example).
Boot VM → Resize partition:
sudo growpart /dev/sda 1
sudo resize2fs /dev/sda1

Steps (Convert):

Convert formats with qemu-img:
qemu-img convert -f qcow2 -O vdi ubuntu_vm.qcow2 ubuntu_vm.vdi
qemu-img convert -f qcow2 -O vpc ubuntu_vm.qcow2 ubuntu_vm.vhd
qemu-img convert -f qcow2 -O raw ubuntu_vm.qcow2 ubuntu_vm.img

Note the result: Image creation, resizing, and conversion completed successfully.

6) Transfer of Files from One Virtual Machine to Another
Aim: Transfer files between Ubuntu and Kali VMs using multiple methods.
Method 1 – NFS:

Set both VMs to Host-Only Adapter (same subnet) + NAT (for package install).
On Ubuntu (server):
sudo apt install nfs-kernel-server -y
sudo mkdir -p /srv/nfs/sharedfolder
sudo chown nobody:nogroup /srv/nfs/sharedfolder
sudo chmod 777 /srv/nfs/sharedfolder
Edit /etc/exports: add /srv/nfs/sharedfolder <Kali_IP>(rw,sync,no_subtree_check,no_root_squash)
sudo exportfs -a && sudo systemctl restart nfs-kernel-server
On Kali (client):
sudo apt install nfs-common -y
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/nfs/sharedfolder
sudo mount <Ubuntu_IP>:/srv/nfs/sharedfolder /mnt/nfs/sharedfolder
Test read/write from both sides.

Method 2 – VirtualBox Shared Folders:

Create folder on host (e.g., C:\VMShared).
For each VM: Settings → Shared Folders → Add → Path, Name, Auto-mount.
Install Guest Additions inside each VM.
Access at /media/sf_VMShared.
Fix permissions: sudo usermod -aG vboxsf $USER → reboot.

Method 3 – SSH/SCP:

Install: sudo apt install openssh-server -y on both.
From client: ssh user@server_IP
scp file.txt user@server_IP:/home/user/

Method 4 – FTP (vsftpd):

On server: sudo apt install vsftpd -y → edit /etc/vsftpd.conf (enable local/write) → restart.
On client: ftp server_IP → use get/put.

Result: All methods work; files can be read and written between VMs.
7a) Configuration of Hyper-V on Windows
Aim: Enable Hyper-V role on Windows Server/Client.
Steps:

Open Server Manager → Add Roles and Features.
Role-based installation → Select server → Next.
Check Hyper-V → Add Features.
Configure virtual switches (Ethernet + any Host-Only if needed).
Choose migration authentication (CredSSP).
Install → Reboot if required.
Open Hyper-V Manager from Tools.
Note the result: Hyper-V configured successfully.

7b) Creation of VM, PowerShell Commands, Management
Aim: Create and manage VMs with GUI and PowerShell.
GUI Steps:

Hyper-V Manager → Right-click server → New → Virtual Machine.
Name + location → Assign memory (e.g., 2 GB) → Connect virtual switch → Create VHD → Attach ISO → Finish.
Start VM and install OS.

PowerShell Examples:

Create VM: New-VM -Name "MyVM" -MemoryStartupBytes 2GB -NewVHDPath "C:\VMs\MyVM.vhdx" -NewVHDSizeBytes 40GB
Add switch: Connect-VMNetworkAdapter -VMName "MyVM" -SwitchName "ExternalSwitch"
Start: Start-VM -Name "MyVM"
Stop: Stop-VM -Name "MyVM"
List: Get-VM
Migrate: Right-click VM → Move → Follow wizard.

Result: VM creation and management (GUI + PowerShell) completed successfully.
8) Installation of OpenStack and Creation of Virtual Machines
Aim: Install OpenStack (DevStack) and launch a VM.
Steps:

sudo apt update
Install required packages for building OpenStack.
Create user stack, give sudo privileges, add to groups.
Set passwordless sudo for stack user.
Switch to stack user: sudo su - stack
Clone DevStack: git clone https://opendev.org/openstack/devstack
cd devstack
Create/edit local.conf and set passwords (ADMIN_PASSWORD, DATABASE_PASSWORD, etc.).
Run: ./stack.sh (takes a long time).
After success, note the Horizon IP shown.
Browse to the IP → Login with admin credentials from local.conf.
Create VM via CLI:
source openrc admin admin
openstack image list / openstack flavor list
openstack keypair create mykey > mykey.pem → chmod 600 mykey.pem
openstack network list
openstack server create --image <image> --flavor <flavor> --nic net-id=<net_id> --key-name mykey my-first-vm
openstack server list
SSH: ssh -i mykey.pem cirros@<VM_IP>

Note the result: OpenStack installed and VM created successfully.
